        {
          "id": "MOMOTUS_BAHAMENSIS",
          "name": "Momotus bahamensis",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "Trinidad Motmot",
          "lineage": "Momotus",
          "continents": [
            "Europe"
          ],
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The Trinidad and Tobago motmot (Momotus bahamensis) is a colourful Coraciiform afroavian endemic to the forests and woodlands of Trinidad and Tobago. It is a nonmigratory member of the Momotidae family and the Momotus genus. This species and the blue-capped motmot, Lesson's motmot, whooping motmot, Amazonian motmot, and Andean motmot were all considered conspecific. Though found on both islands, this bird is more abundant in Tobago than it is in Trinidad.\nThese birds are characterised by their colourful plumage, heavy bills, and low owl-like \"hoot\" call. Their underparts are a deep, rufous colour, with a slightly paler breast and an olive-green wash. The black eye-mask and blue-black racquet tails are also distinguishable features for this species.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "Trinidad motmot",
